1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,011
Every answer the twin gives can cost money.

2
00:00:03,061 --> 00:00:05,613
So it has a ceiling: five dollars a day.

3
00:00:07,440 --> 00:00:09,808
Every question falls through the same layers.

4
00:00:09,858 --> 00:00:14,593
The gateway reads the kill switch first, and if it can't read it, it assumes the worst.

5
00:00:16,040 --> 00:00:17,862
Local answers cost nothing.

6
00:00:17,912 --> 00:00:22,560
Paid ones are metered to the millionth of a dollar, in the gateway's own database.

7
00:00:25,540 --> 00:00:29,030
At three fifty, a warning goes out on Telegram and email.

8
00:00:30,040 --> 00:00:31,351
At five dollars...

9
00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:33,947
it stops itself.

10
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:39,468
One transaction sets the switch, opens an incident, and queues the alert.

11
00:00:39,518 --> 00:00:43,605
Every request after that gets one line, local answers included.

12
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:46,487
"The twin can't talk right now.

13
00:00:46,537 --> 00:00:49,016
Today's budget for its answers is used."

14
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:51,778
A new day doesn't bring it back.

15
00:00:51,828 --> 00:00:56,567
Only an operator reset does: a person, with a reason, in the audit log.

16
00:00:57,640 --> 00:01:02,416
The breach test trips it on purpose: fifty-two assertions, fifty-two passed.

17
00:01:03,640 --> 00:01:10,556
From outside, there's no door: no inbound ports, only an outbound tunnel, and only the gateway has a road out.

18
00:01:11,640 --> 00:01:14,180
The agents that built it are fenced in too.

19
00:01:14,230 --> 00:01:16,151
The one that writes never reviews.

20
00:01:16,201 --> 00:01:20,114
None can touch the budgets or the switch, push, merge, or apply.

21
00:01:20,164 --> 00:01:22,565
And only Hareet runs the final gate.

22
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:28,616
A five-dollar-a-day kill switch. Built in the open, at hareet dot A I.
